    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\nNew John F. Kennedy Aircraft Carrier Launches and Recovers F‑35C Jets for the First Time\n8 photos\nPhoto: Lockheed Martin\nBack in the middle of October, an undisclosed patch of water somewhere near the U.S. saw something unique unfolding: for the first time, two Lockheed Martin F‑35C Lightning II fighter jets repeatedly took off and landed on the deck of the newest aircraft carrier in the world. It was, as the parties involved said when they announced the achievement this week, the start of a new chapter in naval aviation.\nThe PCU John F. Kennedy (CVN 79, soon-to-be USS John F. Kennedy) is by all accounts and purposes, the newest ship of this kind in the world. Part of the new Gerald R. Ford-class, it is currently undergoing final qualification testing, and that includes the launches and recoveries I mentioned above.\nThat's something any new aircraft carrier needs to do, so they all go through such things, but the tests seem even more important for the Kennedy. That's because it debuts a completely new operating environment, centered around a revised Electromagnetic Aircraft Launch System (EMALS) meant to send aircraft on their way in a more controlled manner, and the Advanced Arresting Gear (AAG) that catches them more effectively.\nAs if that wasn’t enough, there is also a redesigned flight deck and island at play on this ship, one that changes airflow, exhaust dynamics, and deck operations.\nWe are told that last month the two F-35s , dispatched from Naval Air Station Patuxent River, Maryland, were at it, testing all of the above for 11 days in a row. During this time, they performed 34 flights, 122 catapult launches, and 122 arrested landings, all of them to perfection.\nThe tests performed in October went far beyond the Kennedy's ability to launch and recover its planes, as they went deeper into checking software and shipboard installations of the EMALS and AAG, but also the loads and structural models for the launch and recovery of the F-35.\nThe John F. Kennedy is the second aircraft being developed in the Gerald R. Ford-class – the first one, wearing the name of the class, was commissioned back in 2017 - and it is presently still undergoing sea trials, with its actual entry into service expected sometime next year.\nThe massive ship can accommodate 4,660 crew and up to 90 combat aircraft. Because it is nuclear-powered, it has the ability to stay at sea indefinitely, as long as it's constantly supplied with food and fuel for the jets. Its pair of nuclear reactors and four shafts push it over the waves at speeds of 35 mph (56 kph).\nThe Kennedy may be the second carrier of the Ford class, but it won't be the last. Work is ongoing on another four, named Enterprise (CVN 80), Doris Miller (CVN 81, William J. Clinton (CVN 82), and George W. Bush (CVN 83). Another four carries on top of these are yet to be named.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nUSS John F. Kennedy aircraft carrier Lockheed Martin f-33\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Google Will Place Solar-Powered AI Data Centers in Space Using the Blue Origin New Glenn Rocket",
